From 8308bc232ac8cf2f9ad4d808a0f09cc0f9ab8e6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slopdealer <326519127+slopdealer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 10:41:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/49] F5: restore keeps a bounded list of tree IDs, not one
 for every directory

Release check F5 (checks/FAILURES.md). The first pass of a restore
recorded the ID of every tree it visited, for the second pass's tree
prefetch: 32 bytes for every directory of the snapshot, more while the
slice was regrown. Peak memory grew by about 104 bytes per directory
(2,020,200 directories: 231 MiB where stock needs 62). M22 had bounded
the other per-directory structure and left this one.

The list now holds at most 65,536 tree IDs (2 MiB) and never allocates
room for more (restorer/tree_id_list.go). It is a prefix of the trees
in the order both passes visit them. The prefetch is unchanged: it
prepares the trees on its list; when the second pass asks for a tree
behind the end of the list, the prefetch's queue is closed and the pass
loads that tree itself, with the same loader, when it needs it. That
path already existed (restoreTreePrefetch.Load) for a second pass that
visits more trees than the first. A snapshot with up to 65,536
directories restores exactly as before; a larger one loses the
read-ahead for the trees past the bound and nothing else.

The list became a field of the Restorer (it was a local of RestoreTo)
so that its size can be observed, with a bound a test can lower; it is
released when RestoreTo returns.

Tests (tree_id_list_test.go):
- the list: never more IDs and never more capacity than its limit; 2
  MiB after 2,020,200 additions with the production bound;
- TestRestoreKeepsBoundedTreeIDList, in the style of the M22 test: a
  restore of 12,041 trees with the bound lowered to 100 never holds
  more than 100 IDs, counted from SelectFilter for every node of both
  passes; with the production bound it holds all 12,041. Without the
  bound it reads 12,041 of 100 and fails;
- TestRestoreAcrossTreeIDBoundEqualsRestoreBelowIt: one snapshot (31
  trees, files, empty files, hard links inside and across directories,
  a symlink where the platform has them) restored with the production
  bound and with ten bounds from 1 to one above the number of trees,
  in ten scenarios: no fault with and without the tree carry; one or
  three trees failing to load in the second pass with the restore
  continuing or stopping; trees damaged behind their second node; the
  root tree failing. Compared: every reported error and its order, the
  returned error, the file count, and every entry of the target (kind,
  permissions, size, content, whether its modification time is the
  snapshot's, which path it is hard-linked to). With the tree behind
  the list handed out empty instead of loaded the test fails.

